}

long_help() {
cat <<'HELP'
PLATFORMS

  macOS    launchd user agent (~/Library/LaunchAgents)
  Linux    systemd --user unit (~/.config/systemd/user), `loginctl enable-linger`
           recommended so the memory server survives logout
  WSL2     same as Linux when systemd is enabled (systemd=true in /etc/wsl.conf,
           then `wsl --shutdown`). Without systemd there is no supervisor, so
           the server must be started manually — `agent-tools doctor` says so.
  Windows  graphify works (pure Python). agentmemory does NOT install cleanly:
           upstream ships no PowerShell/scoop/winget installer for its engine,
           and `agentmemory connect` is unsupported on native Windows. Use WSL2.
           Exception: `agentmemory connect copilot-cli` is Windows-safe.

THE THREE TIERS

  agent-tools init             code only (AST). Free, ~1 min. What a new
                               project gets, so setup never turns into a
                               surprise multi-hour LLM job.
  agent-tools refresh          code + docs + images, then community naming.
                               Incremental: you pay only for docs you edited.
  agent-tools refresh --deep   same, with aggressive inferred-edge extraction.
                               Its cache is a separate namespace, so the first
                               deep run re-reads every doc regardless of what a
                               standard pass already cached.
  scripts/graphify-full-run.sh deep, detached, with retry-on-rate-limit and
                               self-termination. Use this instead of --deep
                               when the corpus is big enough to run for hours.

WHY EACH DEFAULT EXISTS  (all observed 2026-07-28)

graphify
  * Code NEVER goes through an LLM. Extraction with Opus, Sonnet, Haiku and
    --mode deep produced byte-identical code graphs. Only docs/images cost
    tokens. So `graphify update .` is free; doc extraction is the paid part.
  * LAW: labels run LAST, and only via `graphify label`. Labels are keyed to a
    clustering, so anything that changes the graph invalidates them.
    `cluster-only` does NOT call the LLM — it renames every community after its
    hub node and exits 0, which looks like success. Measured erosion from single
    out-of-order runs: 501/535, 280/280 and 137/271 communities hub-renamed.
  * Install needs extras. [gemini] is a MISNOMER — it installs the `openai` HTTP
    client + tiktoken, which is exactly what the gemini, openai, kimi AND ollama
    backends all use (they all speak the OpenAI-compatible protocol). Without it
    any of them dies with "the 'openai' package is required". [mcp] is for the
    MCP server. The only backend not covered is `anthropic` (the API-key Claude
    backend) — add it if you ever want that; `claude-cli` needs no package since
    it shells out to the CLI.
  * There is NO codex backend. graphify's list is: claude, claude-cli, gemini,
    openai, kimi, deepseek, ollama, azure, bedrock. A ChatGPT subscription
    cannot drive it, and neither can Antigravity/`agy`.
  * Antigravity replaces the Gemini *CLI* (sunset 2026-06-18), NOT the Gemini
    API. graphify's gemini backend calls generativelanguage.googleapis.com, so
    the CLI sunset does not affect it.
  * LAW: pin mcp<2. graphify 0.9.29 does `from mcp.types import AnyUrl`, removed
    in mcp 2.0.0, and reports the misleading "mcp not installed" when it IS.
  * Hooks must call bare `graphify`, never an absolute path, or they break on
    the next machine.

agentmemory
  * LAW: the store path is CWD-RELATIVE (v0.9.28: ./data/state_store.db).
    AGENTMEMORY_DATA_DIR and --data-dir are documented but INERT (both tested).
    The service's working directory is the only thing keeping one store. Start
    it somewhere else and you silently get a new, empty one.
  * LAW: `--tools core` must be a server ARGUMENT. Neither ~/.agentmemory/.env
    nor the MCP shim's environment honored AGENTMEMORY_TOOLS. As an argument it
    cuts 53 tools (~5,918 tokens of schema per session) down to 8 (~995).
  * Stop the server before copying store files, or the live engine overwrites
    the session index mid-copy. Store files are JSON + an 8-byte hash/length
    footer; hand-edit them and the engine rejects the file.
  * Never run memory_heal on a freshly restored store — restored observations
    look exactly like the "orphaned" data it deletes.

MCP scope
  * Project scope (-s local), not user scope. A user-scope entry makes every
    `claude -p` subprocess boot the server (~300 ms each), pure waste for
    scripted fan-out.

Git
  * graphify-out/ is generated. Commit ONLY cache/semantic-deep/ and the label
    files: that is the expensive LLM work, and it makes a fresh clone rebuild
    the graph for free. Everything else is derived output and pure churn.
  * The post-commit hook is OPT-IN (fail closed). It began as opt-out, the
    sentinel vanished, and the hook silently re-enabled itself.
HELP
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Package installation, per platform.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Root has no sudo in containers/CI/minimal VMs; non-root without sudo cannot
# install system packages at all. Found by running this on a bare Ubuntu image,
# where `sudo apt-get` failed with "sudo: command not found".
as_root() {
    if [ "$(id -u)" = "0" ]; then "$@"; return; fi
    have sudo || return 127                      # 127 = no sudo at all
    # Passwordless sudo: just go.
    if sudo -n true 2>/dev/null; then sudo "$@"; return; fi
    # Needs a password — only attempt it when stdin is a REAL terminal.
    # `{ : < /dev/tty; }` is not sufficient: over ssh+wsl that open succeeds
    # even with nobody attached, so sudo sat there until "sudo: timed out"
    # (observed on real WSL2 2026-07-29). `[ -t 0 ]` is the honest test.
    if [ -t 0 ]; then sudo "$@"; return; fi
    return 126                                   # 126 = needs password, no tty
}

# agentmemory declares engines: node >=20. npm only WARNS about that, so it
# installs happily on Node 18 and then crash-loops with "does not provide an
# export named 'styleText'" (node:util gained styleText in 20.12). Ubuntu 24.04
# — the most common WSL LTS — ships Node 18.19, so this is not a corner case.
# Observed on a pristine Ubuntu 24.04 WSL2 distro 2026-07-29.
NODE_MIN=20
node_major() { node -v 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^v//; s/[.].*//'; }
node_ok() { have_native node && [ "$(node_major)" -ge "$NODE_MIN" ] 2>/dev/null; }

# Distro packages are often too old; NodeSource is the usual remedy on
# Debian/Ubuntu. Behind confirm() because it adds a third-party apt repo.
install_node_modern() {
    have apt-get || return 1
    have curl || return 1
    curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x 2>/dev/null | as_root bash - >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    as_root apt-get install -y nodejs >/dev/null 2>&1
    node_ok
}

install_node() {
    node_ok && return 0
    have_native node && return 0
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos) have brew && brew install node ;;
        linux|wsl)
            if   have apt-get; then as_root apt-get update -qq && as_root apt-get install -y nodejs npm
            elif have dnf;     then as_root dnf install -y nodejs npm
            elif have pacman;  then as_root pacman -S --noconfirm nodejs npm
            elif have zypper;  then as_root zypper install -y nodejs npm
            else c_bad "no known package manager — install Node 18+ manually"; return 1; fi
            local rc=$?
            if ! have_native node; then
                case $rc in
                  126) c_warn "sudo needs a password and there is no terminal here."
                       c_warn "Run this yourself, then re-run agent-tools:"
                       c_warn "    sudo apt-get install -y nodejs npm" ;;
                  127) c_warn "no sudo available and not root — install Node yourself." ;;
                  *)   c_warn "node install did not succeed — install it yourself: https://nodejs.org" ;;
                esac
            fi ;;
        windows) c_bad "install Node from https://nodejs.org (or: winget install OpenJS.NodeJS)"; return 1 ;;
    esac
    have_native node
}

install_uv() {
    have uv && return 0
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos) have brew && brew install uv && return 0 ;;
    esac
    # Astral's installer is the portable route on Linux/WSL and works on macOS.
    if have curl; then curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh >/dev/null 2>&1; fi
    export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH"
    have uv || { c_bad "install uv manually: https://docs.astral.sh/uv/"; return 1; }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Service management, per platform. Each returns non-zero if unsupported.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
svc_install() {
    local ambin; ambin="$(command -v agentmemory || echo "$BIN/agentmemory")"
    mkdir -p "$AM/logs"
    case "$PLATFORM" in
    macos)
        mkdir -p "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents"
        cat > "$PLIST" <<PLISTEOF
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>$SERVICE_LABEL</string>
  <!-- LAW: --tools core must be an ARGUMENT (env/.env are ignored). 53->8 tools. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array><string>$ambin</string><string>--tools</string><string>core</string></array>
  <!-- LAW: the store path is cwd-relative. Change this and you get an empty store. -->
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>$AM</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key><dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>$BIN:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>HOME</key><string>$HOME</string>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key><dict><key>SuccessfulExit</key><false/></dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>$AM/logs/server.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>$AM/logs/server.err.log</string>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
PLISTEOF
        launchctl bootout "gui/$(id -u)/$SERVICE_LABEL" 2>/dev/null
        launchctl bootstrap "gui/$(id -u)" "$PLIST" 2>/dev/null
        return 0 ;;
    linux|wsl)
        if ! has_systemd; then return 2; fi   # 2 = no supervisor available
        mkdir -p "$UNIT_DIR"
        cat > "$UNIT" <<UNITEOF
[Unit]
Description=agentmemory server
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
# LAW: --tools core must be an ARGUMENT (env and .env are ignored). 53->8 tools.
ExecStart=$ambin --tools core
# LAW: the store path is cwd-relative. Change this and you get an empty store.
WorkingDirectory=$AM
Environment=PATH=$BIN:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
Environment=HOME=$HOME
Restart=on-failure
StandardOutput=append:$AM/logs/server.out.log
StandardError=append:$AM/logs/server.err.log

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
UNITEOF
        systemctl --user daemon-reload 2>/dev/null
        systemctl --user enable --now agentmemory.service 2>/dev/null
        # Without lingering the service dies at logout on most distros.
        have loginctl && loginctl enable-linger "$(id -un)" 2>/dev/null
        return 0 ;;
    windows) return 3 ;;                       # 3 = platform unsupported
    *) return 3 ;;
    esac
}

svc_stop() {
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos) launchctl bootout "gui/$(id -u)/$SERVICE_LABEL" 2>/dev/null ;;
        linux|wsl) has_systemd && systemctl --user stop agentmemory.service 2>/dev/null ;;
    esac
    ( cd "$AM" 2>/dev/null && agentmemory stop --force >/dev/null 2>&1 )
}

svc_start() {
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos) launchctl bootstrap "gui/$(id -u)" "$PLIST" 2>/dev/null ;;
        linux|wsl) has_systemd && systemctl --user start agentmemory.service 2>/dev/null ;;
    esac
}

svc_describe() {
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos) echo "launchd: $PLIST" ;;
        linux|wsl) has_systemd && echo "systemd --user: $UNIT" || echo "no supervisor (start manually)" ;;
        windows) echo "unsupported on native Windows — use WSL2" ;;
    esac
}

# Verify the service definition still encodes both LAWs, whatever the platform.
svc_check() {
    local f=""
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos) f="$PLIST" ;;
        linux|wsl) f="$UNIT" ;;
        *) c_warn "no service on this platform ($PLATFORM)"; return ;;
    esac
    if [ ! -f "$f" ]; then c_warn "no service installed (server won't start at login)"; return; fi
    grep -q "$AM" "$f" && c_ok "LAW: working directory pinned to $AM" \
        || c_bad "LAW VIOLATED: working directory is not $AM — you will get an empty store"
    grep -q "core" "$f" && c_ok "--tools core (8 tools, ~995 tokens)" \
        || c_warn "--tools core not set: 53 tools ~= 5,918 tokens per session"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
graph_stats() {
    [ -n "$PY" ] || { echo "python missing"; return; }
    "$PY" -c "
import json
try:
    d=json.load(open('graphify-out/graph.json'))
    print(f\"{len(d['nodes'])} nodes, {len(d['links'])} edges\")
except FileNotFoundError: print('none yet — run: agent-tools init')
except Exception: print('unreadable')" 2>/dev/null
}

doctor() {
    hdr "Platform"
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos) c_ok "macOS — full support" ;;
        linux) c_ok "Linux — full support" ;;
        wsl)   has_systemd && c_ok "WSL2 with systemd — full support" \
                || c_warn "WSL without systemd: no supervisor. Enable it (systemd=true in /etc/wsl.conf, then 'wsl --shutdown'), or start the server manually." ;;
        windows) c_warn "native Windows — graphify only; agentmemory needs WSL2 (upstream limitation)" ;;
        *) c_warn "unrecognized platform: $(uname -s)" ;;
    esac
    echo "  service: $(svc_describe)"

    hdr "Machine"
    if node_ok; then c_ok "node $(node -v)"
    elif have_native node; then c_bad "node $(node -v) too old — agentmemory needs >= $NODE_MIN (crash-loops on 18)"
    elif have node; then c_bad "node is the WINDOWS one ($(where_is node)) — install Node inside WSL"
    else c_bad "node missing — run: agent-tools install-machine"; fi
    have uv   && c_ok "uv $(uv --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}')" || c_bad "uv missing"
    if [ -n "$PY" ]; then c_ok "python: $PY"
    else c_bad "no working python — 'uv python install' fixes it (Windows: python3 is often a Store stub)"; fi
    if have graphify; then
        c_ok "graphify $(graphify --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}')"
        local gpy="$HOME/.local/share/uv/tools/graphifyy/bin/python"
        [ -x "$gpy" ] || gpy="$(command -v python3)"
        if "$gpy" -c "import mcp.types as t; raise SystemExit(0 if hasattr(t,'AnyUrl') else 1)" 2>/dev/null; then
            c_ok "graphify MCP deps OK (mcp<2 pinned)"
        else
            c_warn "graphify-mcp unavailable — fix: uv tool install \"graphifyy[gemini,mcp]\" --with \"mcp<2\" --force"
        fi
    else c_bad "graphify missing"; fi
    if [ "$PLATFORM" = windows ]; then
        c_warn "agentmemory skipped on native Windows (use WSL2)"
    else
        if have_native agentmemory; then c_ok "agentmemory $(agentmemory --version 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
        elif have agentmemory; then c_bad "agentmemory resolves to a WINDOWS binary: $(where_is agentmemory) — install it inside WSL"
        else c_bad "agentmemory missing"; fi
        if [ "$PLATFORM" = wsl ] && have npm && ! have_native npm; then
            c_bad "npm is the WINDOWS one ($(where_is npm)) — 'sudo apt-get install -y nodejs npm' inside WSL"
        fi
    fi

    hdr "Memory backend: $MEMORY_BACKEND"
    if cm_active && am_active; then
        c_bad "BOTH backends are ACTIVELY CAPTURING."
        c_bad "Every tool call fires two hook sets into two stores — double"
        c_bad "overhead and duplicated memory."
        c_warn "Stop one:"
        c_warn "    claude plugin disable agentmemory@agentmemory --scope local"
        c_warn "    (or)  npx claude-mem stop"
    elif cm_installed && am_installed; then
        # Both present, one inert. That is a legitimate setup: the inactive one
        # is an archive you can still consult by re-enabling it.
        if cm_active; then c_ok "agentmemory installed but INACTIVE (archive) — no conflict"
        elif am_active; then c_ok "claude-mem installed but INACTIVE — no conflict"
        else c_warn "both installed, neither active"; fi
    fi
    case "$MEMORY_BACKEND" in
      claude-mem)
        cm_installed && c_ok "claude-mem installed" || c_bad "claude-mem missing — run: agent-tools install-machine"
        [ -d "$HOME/.claude-mem" ] && c_ok "store: ~/.claude-mem" || c_warn "no store yet at ~/.claude-mem"
        # Correction (2026-07-29): the installer says "Worker autostart skipped
        # — start it manually with npx claude-mem start", so a worker IS wanted.
        # It is lighter than agentmemory's supervised server, but not nothing.
        if curl -sf -m 3 http://127.0.0.1:37701 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            c_ok "worker responding on :37701"
        else
            c_warn "worker not responding — start it:  npx claude-mem start"
        fi ;;
      none) c_warn "no memory backend selected (--memory=none)" ;;
    esac
    if [ -f "$AT_CONFIG" ]; then
        c_ok "backend choice recorded in $AT_CONFIG (machine-wide, all projects)"
    else
        c_warn "backend not recorded — inheriting the default '$MEMORY_DEFAULT'"
        c_warn "    run 'agent-tools init' in any project to pin it"
    fi
    case x in x) ;;
    esac

    hdr "caveman (output compression)"
    if caveman_installed; then
        local _lvl; _lvl="$(caveman_level)"
        if [ -n "$_lvl" ]; then
            c_ok "installed and ACTIVE at level '$_lvl'"
            echo "        replies compress from message one; /caveman off to stop"
        else
            c_ok "installed, currently off (/caveman to switch on)"
        fi
        [ -d "$HOME/.agents/skills/caveman" ] \
            && c_ok "global skills present (all agents)" \
            || c_warn "no global skills — non-Claude agents lack it: agent-tools caveman install"
    else
        c_warn "caveman not installed — agent-tools caveman install"
    fi

    if [ "$MEMORY_BACKEND" = agentmemory ] && [ "$PLATFORM" != windows ]; then
        hdr "Memory server"
        if curl -sf -m 5 http://localhost:3111/agentmemory/health >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            c_ok "responding on :3111"
        else c_bad "not responding on :3111"; fi
        svc_check
        if [ -f "$AM/bin/iii" ] && have file && ! file "$AM/bin/iii" 2>/dev/null | grep -qE "Mach-O|ELF"; then
            c_bad "iii engine binary looks foreign — delete it, it re-downloads"
        fi
    fi

    hdr "Instruction file hygiene"
    # An instruction file is read into context EVERY session, so its size is a
    # permanent tax. Memory backends exist precisely so narrative does not have
    # to live here. Advisory only — this never edits your file.
    local ifile="" f
    for f in CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md .cursorrules; do
        [ -f "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)/$f" ] && { ifile="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/$f"; break; }
    done
    if [ -z "$ifile" ]; then
        c_warn "no CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md in this project"
    else
        local lines bytes longest
        lines="$(wc -l < "$ifile" | tr -d ' ')"
        bytes="$(wc -c < "$ifile" | tr -d ' ')"
        longest="$(awk '{print length}' "$ifile" 2>/dev/null | sort -rn | head -1)"
        if [ "$bytes" -gt 10240 ] || [ "$lines" -gt 200 ]; then
            c_bad "$(basename "$ifile"): $lines lines, $((bytes/1024)) KB — this is read EVERY session"
            c_warn "Keep it to build/test/lint commands and hard constraints."
            c_warn "Narrative, history and decisions belong in memory ($MEMORY_BACKEND),"
            c_warn "long-form docs in docs/. Nothing here is edited automatically."
        else
            c_ok "$(basename "$ifile"): $lines lines, $((bytes/1024)) KB — lean"
        fi
        [ "${longest:-0}" -gt 2000 ] && c_warn "longest line is $longest chars — likely a pasted log or status dump"
    fi

    hdr "Project: $(basename "$(pwd)")"
    if git rev-parse --show-toplevel >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        local root; root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
        [ -f "$root/graphify-out/graph.json" ] && c_ok "graph: $(graph_stats)" || c_warn "no graph — run: agent-tools init"
        grep -q '"graphify hook-guard' "$root/.claude/settings.json" 2>/dev/null \
            && c_ok "hook-guard wired (portable form)" || c_warn "no hook-guard in .claude/settings.json"
        # `graphify claude install` writes its own "## graphify" section. We write
        # a managed block that already covers it, so running both leaves CLAUDE.md
        # saying the same thing twice -- in the very file we ask to keep lean.
        for _instr in "$root/CLAUDE.md" "$root/AGENTS.md"; do
            [ -f "$_instr" ] || continue
            if grep -q "agent-tools:managed" "$_instr" 2>/dev/null \
               && grep -q "^## graphify$" "$_instr" 2>/dev/null; then
                c_warn "$(basename "$_instr") has BOTH graphify's own section and ours"
                echo "        graphify claude uninstall   # drops its section (keeps our hook-guard)"
            fi
        done
        if [ -f "$root/.git/hooks/post-commit" ] && grep -q graphify "$root/.git/hooks/post-commit" 2>/dev/null; then
            [ -f "$root/.git/graphify-auto-update-ENABLED" ] && c_warn "auto-update ENABLED" || c_ok "auto-update installed, disabled (default)"
        else c_warn "no post-commit hook"; fi
        [ -f "$root/.git/hooks/post-checkout" ] && grep -q graphify "$root/.git/hooks/post-checkout" 2>/dev/null \
            && c_ok "post-checkout hook installed (branch switches)" \
            || c_warn "no post-checkout hook — re-run: agent-tools init"
        if have claude; then
            claude mcp list 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^graphify" && c_ok "graphify MCP wired" || c_warn "graphify MCP not wired"
            # Only agentmemory needs an MCP entry of ours. claude-mem registers
            # its own, so "agentmemory MCP not wired" under claude-mem is the
            # CORRECT state -- reporting it as a warning trains people to ignore
            # doctor output.
            case "$MEMORY_BACKEND" in
              agentmemory)
                claude mcp list 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^agentmemory" \
                    && c_ok "agentmemory MCP wired" || c_warn "agentmemory MCP not wired" ;;
              claude-mem)
                if claude mcp list 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^agentmemory"; then
                    c_warn "stale agentmemory MCP entry (backend is claude-mem)"
                    echo "        claude mcp remove agentmemory -s local"
                else c_ok "memory MCP: claude-mem registers its own"; fi ;;
            esac
        fi
    else c_warn "not a git repository"; fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# caveman — output-token compression (https://github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman)
#
# WHY IT IS PART OF THE DEFAULT INSTALL
#   graphify cuts INPUT tokens (ask the graph instead of reading ten files) and
#   the memory backend cuts re-derivation. Neither touches what the agent SAYS
#   BACK, which on a long session is a large share of the spend. caveman is the
#   third leg: a skill telling the agent to drop filler and pleasantries while
#   keeping technical substance and code verbatim. Upstream measures ~65% fewer
#   output tokens; treat that as their number, not ours.
#
#   It is also the cheapest thing here to reverse: it is instructions, not a
#   service. Nothing runs in the background, and turning it off is one command.
#
# WHAT GETS INSTALLED, AND WHY IN TWO STEPS
#   1. caveman's own installer -> the Claude Code PLUGIN (~/.claude/plugins),
#      whose manifest wires SessionStart + UserPromptSubmit hooks. Those hooks
#      are what make it apply from message one rather than only after you type
#      /caveman.
#   2. `skills add -g` -> ~/.agents/skills/caveman*, symlinked into every other
#      agent's directory (Codex, Cursor, Gemini, Continue, ...).
#
#   Step 2 is NOT redundant. caveman's installer does not pass `-g`, so for
#   non-Claude agents it installs PROJECT-scoped into $PWD/.agents/skills --
#   verified 2026-07-29, it dropped skills into the current directory. Run from
#   a temp dir the install evaporates; run from a repo it litters the repo.
#   Hence: always from a neutral directory, and global scope explicitly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CAVEMAN_REPO="JuliusBrussee/caveman"

caveman_level() {          # active level, or empty when off
    local f="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/.caveman-active"
    [ -f "$f" ] && tr -d '[:space:]' < "$f" 2>/dev/null
}

caveman_installed() {
    [ -d "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/plugins/cache/caveman" ] \
        || [ -d "$HOME/.agents/skills/caveman" ]
}

install_caveman() {
    hdr "caveman (output-token compression)"
    if [ "$SKIP_CAVEMAN" = 1 ]; then c_warn "skipped (--no-caveman)"; return 0; fi
    if ! node_ok; then
        c_warn "needs Node >= 18 — skipping caveman (everything else still works)"
        return 0
    fi
    if caveman_installed && [ "$ASSUME_YES" = 0 ] && [ "$NO_INSTALL" = 1 ]; then
        c_ok "caveman already installed (left as-is)"; return 0
    fi
    caveman_installed || confirm "caveman (fewer output tokens; instructions only, no service)" || return 0

    # Run from a directory we do not care about: the upstream installer writes
    # $PWD/.agents/skills for non-Claude agents.
    local neutral; neutral="$(mktemp -d 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}")"
    ( cd "$neutral" && npx -y "github:$CAVEMAN_REPO" --non-interactive >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
        && c_ok "caveman installed for Claude Code (plugin + session hooks)" \
        || c_warn "caveman plugin install failed (network? Node?)"

    # Global, cross-agent. Eve and PromptScript reject global scope by design;
    # that is their limitation and not an error worth surfacing.
    ( cd "$neutral" && npx -y skills add "$CAVEMAN_REPO" --skill '*' --agent '*' -g -y >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
        && c_ok "caveman skills installed globally (~/.agents/skills, all agents)" \
        || c_warn "global skill install failed — run: npx -y skills add $CAVEMAN_REPO --all -g"
    [ -d "$neutral" ] && rm -rf "$neutral" 2>/dev/null

    local lvl; lvl="$(caveman_level)"
    if [ -n "$lvl" ]; then
        c_ok "caveman is ACTIVE at level '$lvl' — replies compress from message one"
    else
        c_ok "installed but idle — run /caveman in a session to switch it on"
    fi
    echo "        levels: lite | full | ultra | wenyan     off: /caveman off"
}

caveman_cmd() {
    case "${1:-status}" in
        install) SKIP_CAVEMAN=0; install_caveman ;;
        status)
            if caveman_installed; then
                local lvl; lvl="$(caveman_level)"
                c_ok "caveman installed${lvl:+ — ACTIVE at level '$lvl'}"
                [ -z "$lvl" ] && c_ok "currently off"
                [ -d "$HOME/.agents/skills/caveman" ] && c_ok "global skills: $(ls "$HOME/.agents/skills" 2>/dev/null | grep -c caveman) present"
                echo
                echo "  In a session:  /caveman [lite|full|ultra|wenyan]   ·   /caveman off"
                echo "  Savings:       /caveman-stats  (upstream's estimate, not measured here)"
            else
                c_warn "not installed — agent-tools caveman install"
            fi ;;
        update|upgrade)
            node_ok || { c_bad "needs Node >= 18"; return 1; }
            local neutral; neutral="$(mktemp -d 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}")"
            ( cd "$neutral" && npx -y "github:$CAVEMAN_REPO" --non-interactive --force >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
                && c_ok "caveman plugin updated" || c_warn "plugin update failed"
            ( cd "$neutral" && npx -y skills update caveman -g -y >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
                && c_ok "global caveman skills updated" || c_warn "skill update failed"
            rm -rf "$neutral" 2>/dev/null ;;
        uninstall|remove)
            node_ok || { c_bad "needs Node >= 18"; return 1; }
            local neutral; neutral="$(mktemp -d 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}")"
            ( cd "$neutral" && npx -y "github:$CAVEMAN_REPO" --uninstall >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
                && c_ok "caveman removed from Claude Code" || c_warn "uninstall reported a problem"
            ( cd "$neutral" && npx -y skills remove caveman -g -y >/dev/null 2>&1 ) >/dev/null 2>&1
            rm -rf "$neutral" 2>/dev/null
            c_ok "done — restart your agent for it to take effect" ;;
        *) c_bad "usage: agent-tools caveman [status|install|update|uninstall]"; return 2 ;;
    esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Updating. Each component updates differently and some have footguns, so a
# single command is safer than asking people to remember four incantations.
#
#   graphify    MUST be reinstalled with extras + the mcp<2 pin re-specified,
#               or you silently lose the gemini backend / the MCP server.
#   claude-mem  its own installer; the worker must be restarted afterwards.
#   agentmemory npm global, then the service restarted or you keep running the
#               old binary.
#   caveman     re-run from GitHub (unpinned) + update the global skills.
#   self        git pull + install.sh in the Agent-Tools checkout.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
update_cmd() {
    local what="${1:-all}"
    case "$what" in all|graphify|memory|caveman|self) ;;
        *) c_bad "usage: agent-tools update [all|graphify|memory|caveman|self]"; return 2 ;;
    esac

    if [ "$what" = all ] || [ "$what" = graphify ]; then
        hdr "graphify"
        if have uv; then
            c_warn "an upgrade can invalidate the semantic cache (re-extraction costs hours)"
            uv tool install "graphifyy[gemini,mcp]" --with "mcp<2" --force >/dev/null 2>&1 \
                && c_ok "graphify $(graphify --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}')" \
                || c_bad "graphify update failed"
        else c_warn "uv not installed — skipping"; fi
    fi

    if [ "$what" = all ] || [ "$what" = memory ]; then
        hdr "memory backend ($MEMORY_BACKEND)"
        case "$MEMORY_BACKEND" in
          claude-mem)
            if node_ok; then
                npx -y claude-mem@latest install >/dev/null 2>&1 \
                    && c_ok "claude-mem updated" || c_bad "claude-mem update failed"
                npx claude-mem start >/dev/null 2>&1 \
                    && c_ok "worker restarted" \
                    || c_warn "start the worker yourself: npx claude-mem start"
            else c_warn "needs Node >= $NODE_MIN — skipping"; fi ;;
          agentmemory)
            if node_ok; then
                npm install -g @agentmemory/agentmemory@latest >/dev/null 2>&1 \
                    && c_ok "agentmemory updated" || c_bad "agentmemory update failed"
                # The old binary keeps running until the service restarts.
                # (svc_stop/svc_start, not a restart helper -- there isn't one.)
                svc_stop >/dev/null 2>&1
                if svc_start >/dev/null 2>&1; then c_ok "service restarted"
                else c_warn "restart the service yourself: $(svc_describe)"; fi
            else c_warn "needs Node >= $NODE_MIN — skipping"; fi ;;
          none) c_warn "no memory backend selected" ;;
        esac
    fi

    if [ "$what" = all ] || [ "$what" = caveman ]; then
        caveman_installed && { hdr "caveman"; caveman_cmd update; } \
            || { [ "$what" = caveman ] && c_warn "caveman not installed — agent-tools caveman install"; }
    fi

    if [ "$what" = all ] || [ "$what" = self ]; then
        hdr "agent-tools itself"
        local src; src="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)"
        if [ -d "$src/.git" ]; then
            ( cd "$src" && git pull --ff-only >/dev/null 2>&1 && ./install.sh >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
                && c_ok "agent-tools updated from $src" \
                || c_warn "could not update in place — cd $src && git pull && ./install.sh"
        else
            c_warn "not running from a git checkout; update manually:"
            echo "        cd /path/to/Agent-Tools && git pull && ./install.sh"
        fi
    fi

    echo
    c_ok "done — run 'agent-tools doctor' to confirm nothing came loose"
}

install_machine() {
    hdr "Platform: $PLATFORM"
    [ "$PLATFORM" = windows ] && c_warn "native Windows: graphify only. For agentmemory use WSL2 (upstream)."
    if [ "$PLATFORM" = macos ] && ! have brew; then c_bad "install Homebrew first: https://brew.sh"; exit 1; fi

    hdr "1. Prerequisites"
    if node_ok; then c_ok "node $(node -v)"
    elif have_native node; then
        c_bad "node $(node -v) is too old — agentmemory needs >= $NODE_MIN"
        if confirm "a modern Node from NodeSource (adds their apt repo)"; then
            install_node_modern && c_ok "node $(node -v)" || c_warn "NodeSource failed — use nvm or fnm"
        fi
    elif confirm "Node.js (required for agentmemory; graphify works without it)"; then
        install_node && c_ok "node $(node -v)" || c_warn "node install did not succeed"
    fi
    if have uv; then c_ok "uv $(uv --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}')"
    elif confirm "uv (REQUIRED for graphify)"; then
        install_uv && c_ok "uv $(uv --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}')"
    fi
    have uv || { c_bad "uv is required for graphify — nothing further to do"; exit 1; }

    # agent-tools itself needs a working python (reading the graph, writing
    # .claude/settings.json). graphify does not — uv gives it its own. On
    # Windows a system python is often absent, or is the Microsoft Store stub.
    if [ -z "$PY" ]; then
        c_bad "no working python found (on Windows, python3 is often a Store stub)"
        if confirm "a Python via uv (no admin rights, no extra package manager)"; then
            uv python install >/dev/null 2>&1
            PY="$(_pick_python)"
            [ -n "$PY" ] && c_ok "python ready: $PY" || c_warn "could not provision python — install Python 3 yourself"
        fi
    else
        c_ok "python: $PY"
    fi

    hdr "2. graphify (extras + the mcp<2 pin)"
    if have graphify && [ "$ASSUME_YES" = 0 ] && [ "$NO_INSTALL" = 1 ]; then
        c_ok "graphify $(graphify --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}') (left as-is)"
    elif have graphify || confirm "graphify"; then
        uv tool install "graphifyy[gemini,mcp]" --with "mcp<2" --force >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            && c_ok "graphify installed" || c_bad "graphify install failed"
    fi

    if [ "$MEMORY_BACKEND" = none ]; then
        hdr "3. memory — SKIPPED (--memory=none)"
        c_ok "graphify is set up; no memory backend requested"
        return
    fi

    if [ "$MEMORY_BACKEND" = claude-mem ]; then
        hdr "3. memory backend: claude-mem"
        # Loud warning rather than a block: the user may deliberately keep the
        # other installed but inactive. Two ACTIVE capturers is the problem.
        if am_installed; then
            c_bad "agentmemory is ALSO installed. Do not run both capturing at once:"
            c_bad "  every tool call would fire two hook sets into two stores."
            c_warn "Stop agentmemory's capture before using claude-mem:"
            c_warn "    claude plugin uninstall agentmemory@agentmemory"
            case "$PLATFORM" in
              macos) c_warn "    launchctl bootout gui/\$(id -u)/$SERVICE_LABEL" ;;
              linux|wsl) c_warn "    systemctl --user disable --now agentmemory" ;;
            esac
        fi
        if cm_installed; then c_ok "claude-mem present"
        elif ! have npm; then c_warn "npm missing — cannot install claude-mem"
        elif confirm "claude-mem (memory that persists across sessions)"; then
            npx -y claude-mem install >/dev/null 2>&1 \
              && c_ok "claude-mem installed (it wires its own hooks + MCP)" \
              || c_bad "claude-mem install failed — try: npx claude-mem install"
        fi
        remember_backend claude-mem
        install_caveman
        hdr "Next"
        echo "  cd <your-project> && agent-tools init"
        echo "  claude-mem has NO bulk importer; to replay past sessions see"
        echo "  scripts/claude-mem-import-transcripts.sh (unverified — dry-run first)"
        return
    fi

    # ---- agentmemory path ------------------------------------------------
    if cm_installed; then
        c_bad "claude-mem is ALSO installed. Two active capturers is the problem:"
        c_bad "  every tool call fires two hook sets into two stores."
        c_warn "Remove one before continuing:  npx claude-mem uninstall"
    fi
    remember_backend agentmemory

    # Two different reasons to skip, two different messages — sharing one text
    # printed "use WSL2" on a Linux box, which is nonsense.
    if [ "$PLATFORM" = windows ]; then
        hdr "3. agentmemory — SKIPPED"
        c_warn "native Windows: upstream ships no engine installer here and"
        c_warn "\`agentmemory connect\` is unsupported. Use WSL2 and re-run there."
        return
    fi
    if [ "$PLATFORM" = wsl ] && have npm && ! have_native npm; then
        hdr "3. agentmemory — SKIPPED"
        c_bad "npm here is the WINDOWS one: $(where_is npm)"
        c_warn "Installing through it would put agentmemory in the Windows npm"
        c_warn "prefix, and the Linux service would fail while looking fine."
        c_warn "Install Node INSIDE WSL first, then re-run:"
        c_warn "    sudo apt-get install -y nodejs npm"
        return
    fi
    if have_native node && ! node_ok; then
        hdr "3. agentmemory — SKIPPED"
        c_bad "node $(node -v) < $NODE_MIN — installing here yields a crash-looping service."
        c_warn "Get Node >= $NODE_MIN, then re-run:"
        c_warn "    curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x | sudo bash - && sudo apt-get install -y nodejs"
        return
    fi
    if ! have_native npm; then
        hdr "3. agentmemory — SKIPPED"
        c_warn "npm not available, so agentmemory cannot be installed."
        c_warn "Install Node 18+ and re-run 'agent-tools install-machine'."
        c_warn "graphify above is fully set up and works without it."
        return
    fi

    hdr "3. agentmemory"
    if have_native agentmemory; then c_ok "agentmemory $(agentmemory --version 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
    elif confirm "agentmemory (persistent memory across sessions)"; then
        npm install -g @agentmemory/agentmemory >/dev/null 2>&1
        have agentmemory && c_ok "agentmemory installed" || c_bad "agentmemory install failed"
    else
        c_warn "skipped — graphify above is fully set up and works without it"
        return
    fi
    # A store copied from another OS carries an unusable engine binary.
    if [ -f "$AM/bin/iii" ] && have file && ! file "$AM/bin/iii" 2>/dev/null | grep -qE "Mach-O|ELF"; then
        mkdir -p "$AM/.foreign"; mv "$AM/bin/iii" "$AM/.foreign/" 2>/dev/null
        c_warn "moved foreign iii binary aside; it will re-download"
    fi
    rm -f "$AM/iii.pid" "$AM/worker.pid" "$AM/engine-state.json" 2>/dev/null

    hdr "4. Memory server"
    svc_install; local rc=$?
    case $rc in
        0) c_ok "service installed and started — $(svc_describe)" ;;
        2) c_warn "no systemd here. Start manually (LAW: from $AM, or you get an empty store):"
           echo "        cd $AM && agentmemory --tools core &" ;;
        3) c_warn "no service manager for this platform" ;;
    esac

    hdr "5. Claude Code plugin (the hooks that capture memory)"
    if have claude; then
        if claude plugin list 2>/dev/null | grep -q agentmemory; then c_ok "agentmemory plugin present"
        elif confirm "the agentmemory Claude Code plugin (the hooks that capture memory)"; then
            claude plugin marketplace add rohitg00/agentmemory >/dev/null 2>&1
            claude plugin install agentmemory@agentmemory >/dev/null 2>&1 && c_ok "agentmemory plugin installed"
        fi
    else c_warn "claude CLI not found — install it, then re-run"; fi

    install_caveman
    hdr "Next"
    echo "  cd <your-project> && agent-tools init"
    echo "  MANUAL: memory data does not migrate. See 'agent-tools help'."
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
init_project() {
    local root; root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || { c_bad "not a git repository"; exit 1; }
    cd "$root" || exit 1
    hdr "Project: $root  (platform: $PLATFORM)"

    if ! grep -q "graphify-out/\*" .gitignore 2>/dev/null; then
        cat >> .gitignore <<'GI'

# Graphify knowledge graph. Derived output is ignored; the LLM-derived cache and
# labels ARE committed — that is the expensive part (hours of extraction in a
# couple of MB), and it lets a fresh clone rebuild the graph at zero LLM cost.
# SYNTAX: never use `graphify-out/` or `graphify-out/**` — git will not descend
# into an excluded directory, which silently kills every negation below.
graphify-out/*
!graphify-out/cache/
graphify-out/cache/*
!graphify-out/cache/semantic-deep/
!graphify-out/.graphify_labels.json
!graphify-out/.graphify_labels.json.sig
GI
        c_ok ".gitignore rules added"
    else c_ok ".gitignore already configured"; fi

    mkdir -p .claude
    if ! grep -q "graphify hook-guard" .claude/settings.json 2>/dev/null; then
        "$PY" - <<'PY'
import json, os
p = ".claude/settings.json"
d = {}
if os.path.exists(p):
    try: d = json.load(open(p))
    except Exception: d = {}
hooks = d.setdefault("hooks", {}).setdefault("PreToolUse", [])
have = json.dumps(hooks)
# Bare `graphify` on PATH — an absolute path breaks on the next machine.
for matcher, kind in (("Bash", "search"), ("Read|Glob", "read")):
    if f"hook-guard {kind}" not in have:
        hooks.append({"matcher": matcher,
                      "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": f"graphify hook-guard {kind}"}]})
json.dump(d, open(p, "w"), indent=2)
PY
        c_ok "hook-guard added to .claude/settings.json"
    else c_ok "hook-guard already present"; fi

    if [ ! -f .git/hooks/post-commit ] || ! grep -q graphify .git/hooks/post-commit 2>/dev/null; then
        cat > .git/hooks/post-commit <<'HK'
#!/bin/bash
# graphify auto-update — code only (AST, no LLM, no cost), never blocks a commit.
# OPT-IN by design: absent gate file == disabled. An opt-out guard once vanished
# and silently re-enabled itself, re-clustering the graph after a commit.
REPO="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || exit 0
ENABLE="$REPO/.git/graphify-auto-update-ENABLED"; LOG="$REPO/.git/graphify-auto-update.log"
[ -f "$ENABLE" ] || exit 0
# Skip mid-rebase/merge/cherry-pick: a 20-commit rebase would otherwise fire 20
# rebuilds, and an unstaged graphify-out/ blocks `git rebase --continue`.
GIT_DIR=${GIT_DIR:-$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)}
[ -d "$GIT_DIR/rebase-merge" ] || [ -d "$GIT_DIR/rebase-apply" ] && exit 0
[ -f "$GIT_DIR/MERGE_HEAD" ] || [ -f "$GIT_DIR/CHERRY_PICK_HEAD" ] && exit 0
# Concurrency guard. Deliberately GLOBAL, not repo-scoped: reading another
# process's cwd needs /proc (Linux) or lsof (macOS) and is not portable. A run
# in another repo therefore suppresses this one -- conservative, but it must not
# be SILENT, or the graph quietly stops updating and nobody knows why.
if command -v pgrep >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  if pgrep -f "graphif[y] (extract|update|label|cluster-only|watch)" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "[$(date '+%F %T')] skipped: another graphify run is in flight" >> "$LOG"
    exit 0
  fi
fi
GRAPHIFY="$(command -v graphify || echo "$HOME/.local/bin/graphify")"
[ -x "$GRAPHIFY" ] || exit 0
( cd "$REPO" && echo "[$(date '+%F %T')] update $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)" >> "$LOG" \
  && "$GRAPHIFY" update . >> "$LOG" 2>&1 ) >/dev/null 2>&1 &
exit 0
HK
        chmod +x .git/hooks/post-commit
        c_ok "post-commit hook installed (DISABLED by default)"
    else c_ok "post-commit hook already present"; fi

    # Branch switches change code wholesale, so a graph built on the old branch
    # is stale until the next commit. graphify ships its own post-checkout hook,
    # but `graphify hook install` also writes `.gitattributes` -- a COMMITTED
    # file -- registering a merge driver that only exists in the local
    # .git/config, by absolute path. Teammates would inherit a merge=graphify
    # attribute pointing at a driver they do not have. So we install the useful
    # half ourselves, behind the same opt-in gate.
    if [ ! -f .git/hooks/post-checkout ] || ! grep -q graphify .git/hooks/post-checkout 2>/dev/null; then
        cat > .git/hooks/post-checkout <<'HK'
#!/bin/bash
# graphify auto-update on branch switch — code only (AST, no LLM, no cost).
# OPT-IN: absent gate file == disabled. Same gate as post-commit.
[ "$3" = "1" ] || exit 0                      # $3=1 means branch switch, not file checkout
REPO="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || exit 0
ENABLE="$REPO/.git/graphify-auto-update-ENABLED"; LOG="$REPO/.git/graphify-auto-update.log"
[ -f "$ENABLE" ] || exit 0
[ -d "$REPO/graphify-out" ] || exit 0         # never build a graph from scratch here
GIT_DIR=${GIT_DIR:-$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)}
[ -d "$GIT_DIR/rebase-merge" ] || [ -d "$GIT_DIR/rebase-apply" ] && exit 0
# Concurrency guard. Deliberately GLOBAL, not repo-scoped: reading another
# process's cwd needs /proc (Linux) or lsof (macOS) and is not portable. A run
# in another repo therefore suppresses this one -- conservative, but it must not
# be SILENT, or the graph quietly stops updating and nobody knows why.
if command -v pgrep >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  if pgrep -f "graphif[y] (extract|update|label|cluster-only|watch)" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "[$(date '+%F %T')] skipped: another graphify run is in flight" >> "$LOG"
    exit 0
  fi
fi
GRAPHIFY="$(command -v graphify || echo "$HOME/.local/bin/graphify")"
[ -x "$GRAPHIFY" ] || exit 0
( cd "$REPO" && echo "[$(date '+%F %T')] checkout $(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)" >> "$LOG" \
  && "$GRAPHIFY" update . >> "$LOG" 2>&1 ) >/dev/null 2>&1 &
exit 0
HK
        chmod +x .git/hooks/post-checkout
        c_ok "post-checkout hook installed (DISABLED by default)"
    else c_ok "post-checkout hook already present"; fi

    if have claude; then
        local gmcp; gmcp="$(command -v graphify-mcp || echo "$BIN/graphify-mcp")"
        claude mcp list 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^graphify" \
            && c_ok "graphify MCP already wired" \
            || { claude mcp add graphify -s local -- "$gmcp" >/dev/null 2>&1 && c_ok "graphify MCP wired (10 tools, ~1.5k tokens)"; }
        # Wire the MCP for the SELECTED backend only. claude-mem registers its
        # own MCP during `claude-mem install`, so we must not add a second
        # entry; agentmemory needs one pointed at its server.
        case "$MEMORY_BACKEND" in
          agentmemory)
            if [ "$PLATFORM" != windows ]; then
                claude mcp list 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^agentmemory" \
                    && c_ok "agentmemory MCP already wired" \
                    || { claude mcp add agentmemory -s local -e AGENTMEMORY_URL=http://localhost:3111 -- npx -y @agentmemory/mcp >/dev/null 2>&1 && c_ok "agentmemory MCP wired (8 tools, ~1k tokens)"; }
            fi ;;
          claude-mem)
            c_ok "claude-mem wires its own MCP (nothing to add here)"
            # A leftover entry from a previous backend would keep a dead server
            # in every session's tool list.
            if claude mcp list 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^agentmemory"; then
                c_warn "an agentmemory MCP entry is still wired but the backend is claude-mem"
                c_warn "    claude mcp remove agentmemory -s local"
            fi ;;
        esac
    fi

    # The backend choice is machine-wide (~/.config/agent-tools/config), not
    # per-project. It is written by install-machine and by `memory switch` --
    # but a machine set up by hand never ran either, so the file can be absent
    # and the backend silently inherited from MEMORY_DEFAULT. That works until
    # the default changes under you. Record the effective value once.
    if [ ! -f "$AT_CONFIG" ]; then
        remember_backend "$MEMORY_BACKEND"
        c_ok "recorded memory backend '$MEMORY_BACKEND' in $AT_CONFIG"
    fi

    # --- agent instructions -------------------------------------------------
    # MCP tools alone are passive: an agent CAN call them but often won't. The
    # durable fix is the instruction file the agent already reads every session.
    # CLAUDE.md FIRST when it exists: it is what Claude Code actually reads, and
    # it is the file that bloats — putting a "keep this lean" rule in a
    # different file than the one growing is theatre. AGENTS.md is the
    # cross-agent standard, so it is the fallback and what we create fresh.
    local instr=""
    for f in CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md .cursorrules; do [ -f "$f" ] && { instr="$f"; break; }; done
    [ -z "$instr" ] && instr="AGENTS.md"
    # `graphify claude install` writes its own "## graphify" section covering the
    # same ground. We do not call it (our block is a superset and stays portable),
    # but a user who ran it by hand ends up with both -- duplicate instructions in
    # the one file we ask to keep lean. Say so rather than quietly stacking.
    if grep -q "^## graphify$" "$instr" 2>/dev/null; then
        c_warn "$instr already has graphify's own section; ours repeats it"
        c_warn "    graphify claude uninstall   # then re-run: agent-tools init"
    fi
    if grep -q "agent-tools:managed" "$instr" 2>/dev/null; then
        c_ok "agent instructions already in $instr"
    elif confirm "a short 'use the graph and memory' section in $instr"; then
        cat >> "$instr" <<'INSTR'

<!-- agent-tools:managed -->
## Codebase knowledge (graphify)

Before grepping or reading files broadly, ask the graph:

    graphify query "<question>"        # scoped subgraph, cheap
    graphify affected "<symbol>"       # blast radius before refactoring
    graphify god-nodes                 # what is risky to touch at all
    graphify path "<A>" "<B>"          # how two things connect
    graphify explain "<concept>"       # one concept, focused

If graphify-out/wiki/index.md exists, use it to navigate broadly. Read
GRAPH_REPORT.md only for whole-architecture review — it is large.

The graph covers code structure, docs and concepts. It does NOT index
string-level details (CSS variables, literals) — use grep for those.
If the graph looks stale, run `agent-tools refresh`.

## Memory

Past sessions are searchable through the memory backend. Recall before
re-deriving an earlier decision, and save durable decisions explicitly.
Memory is for decisions and rationale; the graph is for what the code *is*.

## Keep this file lean

This file is read into context on EVERY session, so its size is a permanent
tax on every request. Keep it to things that do not fit anywhere else:

- build / test / lint / run commands
- hard constraints and invariants that must never be violated
- pointers to where the real documentation lives

Do NOT put here: session logs, changelogs, status updates, narrative history,
lists of what was done and when, or anything that reads like a diary. Those
belong in the memory backend (decisions, rationale) or in docs/ (long-form).
If a section is growing over time rather than being edited, it belongs
somewhere else.
<!-- /agent-tools:managed -->
INSTR
        c_ok "agent instructions added to $instr"
    fi

    if [ -f graphify-out/graph.json ]; then
        c_ok "graph exists: $(graph_stats)"
    else
        hdr "Building the code graph (AST only, no LLM, no cost)"
        graphify update . 2>&1 | tail -3
        c_ok "graph built: $(graph_stats)"
        echo
        c_warn "Docs are NOT indexed yet — that pass uses an LLM. When you want it:"
        echo "      agent-tools refresh"
    fi

    hdr "Done"
    echo "  agent-tools doctor    verify"
    echo "  agent-tools refresh   after a batch of work"
    echo "  touch .git/graphify-auto-update-ENABLED   to auto-update on commit"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Run a refresh in the background, surviving the terminal that launched it.
# Mechanism per platform, best available first:
#   Linux/WSL + systemd : systemd-run --user transient unit. Cleans itself up
#                         when the command exits — nothing to unregister.
#   macOS               : caffeinate (so idle sleep cannot pause a long run)
#                         wrapped in nohup, detached from the shell.
#   anything else       : plain nohup.
# The detached child is invoked WITHOUT --detach, or it would detach forever.
detach_refresh() {
    local mode="$1" root="$2"
    local log="$root/.git/graphify-refresh.log"
    [ -d "$root/.git" ] || log="$root/graphify-refresh.log"
    local self; self="$(command -v agent-tools || echo "$0")"

    if graphify_busy_here "$root"; then
        c_bad "a graphify run is already active in this repo: ${BUSY_WHERE:-?}"; return 1
    fi

    hdr "Detaching"
    echo "  mode : ${mode/#full/full (code + docs + naming)}"
    echo "  log  : $log"
    : > "$log"

    case "$PLATFORM" in
      linux|wsl)
        if has_systemd; then
            local unit="graphify-refresh-$$"
            systemd-run --user --unit="$unit" --working-directory="$root" \
                --setenv=PATH="$PATH" \
                bash -c "'$self' refresh $mode >> '$log' 2>&1" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
              && { c_ok "started as systemd transient unit: $unit"
                   _detach_help "journalctl --user -u $unit -f" "systemctl --user stop $unit" "$log"
                   return 0; }
            c_warn "systemd-run failed — falling back to nohup"
        fi ;;
      macos)
        # caffeinate -i keeps the machine awake for the CHILD's lifetime only.
        ( cd "$root" && nohup caffeinate -i -m "$self" refresh $mode >> "$log" 2>&1 & ) >/dev/null 2>&1
        c_ok "started detached (caffeinate keeps the machine awake for it)"
        _detach_help "tail -f '$log'" "agent-tools stop" "$log"
        return 0 ;;
    esac

    ( cd "$root" && nohup "$self" refresh $mode >> "$log" 2>&1 & ) >/dev/null 2>&1
    c_ok "started detached (nohup)"
    _detach_help "tail -f '$log'" "agent-tools stop" "$log"
    return 0
}

# Printed after every successful detach, so the three questions people actually
# have — is it alive, how do I watch it, how do I stop it — are answered without
# reading docs.
_detach_help() {
    local follow="$1" stop="$2" log="$3"
    echo
    echo "  is it running?   agent-tools status"
    echo "  watch progress   $follow"
    echo "  stop it early    $stop"
    echo
    echo "  When it finishes it EXITS on its own — there is no daemon to clean"
    echo "  up and nothing to kill. The log stays at:"
    echo "      $log"
}

refresh() {
    local root; root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || { c_bad "not a git repository"; exit 1; }
    cd "$root" || exit 1

    # Flags may combine: `refresh --deep --detach`. Validate BEFORE doing
    # anything — an unrecognised flag used to fall through to the default case
    # and silently start a full semantic pass, i.e. a typo could kick off an
    # hours-long LLM run.
    local mode=full detach=0 a
    for a in "$@"; do
        case "$a" in
            --code-only) mode="--code-only" ;;
            --deep)      mode="--deep" ;;
            --detach)    detach=1 ;;
            full|"")     ;;
            *) c_bad "unknown option for refresh: $a"
               echo "  valid: (none) | --code-only | --deep | --detach"
               exit 2 ;;
        esac
    done

    # Guard AFTER validation, so a typo reports the typo — not "something else
    # is running" — and scoped to this repo, so an unrelated project's run does
    # not block you.
    if graphify_busy_here "$root"; then
        c_bad "a graphify run is already active in this repo: ${BUSY_WHERE:-?}"
        echo "  wait for it, or stop it, then retry"
        exit 1
    fi

    if [ "$detach" = 1 ]; then
        detach_refresh "$mode" "$root"
        return $?
    fi

    hdr "Before"; echo "  $(graph_stats)"

    hdr "1/3 structure"     # LAW: structure first, always
    case "$mode" in
      --code-only)
        graphify update . || exit 1 ;;
      --deep)
        # Deep mode uses its OWN cache namespace (cache/semantic-deep/), so the
        # first deep run re-dispatches every doc even if a standard pass already
        # cached them. That is the cost driver, not the repo size.
        local ndocs; ndocs="$(find . -name '*.md' -not -path './.git/*' -not -path './graphify-out/*' 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
        c_warn "deep mode: aggressive inferred-edge extraction over docs/images."
        c_warn "Its cache namespace starts EMPTY, so the first run re-reads everything."
        c_warn "~$ndocs markdown files here. On a large corpus this runs for hours —"
        c_warn "for that, use scripts/graphify-full-run.sh (detached, retries, resumes)."
        # Deep chunks are big, and the claude-cli backend answers slowly on them.
        # graphify's default API timeout is 600s; a chunk that exceeds it is lost and
        # the whole run lands PARTIAL, which the node-count guard then (correctly)
        # refuses to write. Observed 2026-07-29: chunk 23/26 timed out, the run produced
        # 3341 nodes against an existing 14647, and graphify refused to overwrite.
        # Raise the ceiling, but never override a value the user set deliberately.
        export GRAPHIFY_API_TIMEOUT="${GRAPHIFY_API_TIMEOUT:-1800}"
        c_warn "API timeout for this run: ${GRAPHIFY_API_TIMEOUT}s per chunk"
        graphify extract . --backend claude-cli --mode deep || exit 1 ;;
      *)
        graphify extract . --backend claude-cli || exit 1 ;;
    esac
    c_ok "$(graph_stats)"

    hdr "2/3 labels"        # LAW: `label`, never `cluster-only`; and always LAST
    if [ "$mode" = "--code-only" ]; then c_warn "skipped (code-only); names may be hub-derived"
    else graphify label . --backend claude-cli >/dev/null 2>&1 && c_ok "communities named" || c_warn "label failed"; fi

    hdr "3/3 verify"
    graphify tree >/dev/null 2>&1 && c_ok "GRAPH_TREE.html written"
    local n total
    n="$(graphify query "architecture overview" 2>/dev/null | grep -c '^NODE')"
    total="$("$PY" -c "import json;print(len(json.load(open('graphify-out/graph.json'))['nodes']))" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    if [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
        c_ok "query returns $n nodes — graph answers"
    elif [ "${total:-0}" -lt 25 ]; then
        # A handful of nodes is a real state for a tiny repo, not a failure.
        c_warn "graph is very small ($total nodes) — nothing to match yet, which is fine"
    else
        c_bad "query returned nothing despite $total nodes — investigate"
    fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Obsidian export: put BOTH halves of what these tools know into one vault.
#
#   graph    -> `graphify export obsidian --dir VAULT/graph`
#               one note per node/community, wiki-linked, so Obsidian's own
#               graph view renders your codebase structure.
#   memories -> memory-export.py, as notes with YAML front matter, tags and
#               [[links]] on shared concepts.
#
# Deliberately ON DEMAND. Nothing writes to your vault unless you ask, because
# a vault is a place people curate by hand and surprise writes are rude. It is
# also a SNAPSHOT: the vault drifts from the live store until you re-run this,
# and the agent still queries graph.json, never your vault.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
obsidian_export() {
    local vault="${1:-}"
    if [ -z "$vault" ]; then
        c_bad "usage: agent-tools obsidian <vault-dir> [--graph-only|--memory-only]"
        echo "  e.g. agent-tools obsidian ~/Documents/MyVault/agent-tools"
        return 2
    fi
    local mode="${2:-both}"
    local root; root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || root="$(pwd)"
    local project; project="$(basename "$root")"

    mkdir -p "$vault" || { c_bad "cannot create $vault"; return 1; }
    hdr "Obsidian export -> $vault"

    local did_graph=0 did_mem=0

    if [ "$mode" != "--memory-only" ]; then
        if [ -f "$root/graphify-out/graph.json" ]; then
            local gdir="$vault/$project-graph"
            if (cd "$root" && graphify export obsidian --dir "$gdir" >/dev/null 2>&1); then
                c_ok "graph -> $gdir ($(find "$gdir" -name '*.md' 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ') notes)"
                did_graph=1
            else
                c_warn "graph export failed (is the graph built? agent-tools init)"
            fi
        else
            c_warn "no graph in this project — skipping graph export"
        fi
    fi

    if [ "$mode" != "--graph-only" ]; then
        # A vault with only the graph is half a second brain: structure without
        # the reasoning behind it. Both backends export, so neither leaves the
        # vault one-sided.
        if [ "$MEMORY_BACKEND" = claude-mem ]; then
            # Reads ~/.claude-mem/claude-mem.db read-only; the worker need not
            # be running, and an export can never mutate the live store.
            memory_export "$vault/memories" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
              && { c_ok "memories -> $vault/memories ($(find "$vault/memories" -name '*.md' 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ') notes)"; did_mem=1; } \
              || c_warn "claude-mem export failed (no store at ~/.claude-mem yet?)"
        elif [ "$MEMORY_BACKEND" = agentmemory ] && curl -sf -m 5 http://localhost:3111/agentmemory/health >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            memory_export "$vault/memories" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
              && { c_ok "memories -> $vault/memories ($(find "$vault/memories" -name '*.md' 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ') notes)"; did_mem=1; } \
              || c_warn "memory export failed"
        else
            c_warn "memory backend not reachable — skipping memories"
        fi
    fi

    # A vault entry point, so the export is navigable rather than a pile.
    {
        printf '# %s — agent-tools export\n\n' "$project"
        printf '_Snapshot taken %s. Re-run `agent-tools obsidian %s` to refresh._\n\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M')" "$vault"
        [ "$did_graph" = 1 ] && printf -- '- [[%s-graph/INDEX|Codebase graph]] — structure, communities, god nodes\n' "$project"
        [ "$did_mem" = 1 ]   && printf -- '- [[memories/INDEX|Memories]] — decisions and rationale\n'
        printf '\n> These notes are a snapshot for humans. Agents query the live\n'
        printf '> graph (`graphify-out/graph.json`) and the live memory store,\n'
        printf '> not this vault.\n'
    } > "$vault/INDEX.md"
    c_ok "vault index -> $vault/INDEX.md"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Memory backend management.
#
# Switching is SYSTEM-WIDE, not per project: both backends hook the session
# lifecycle at user scope and keep one machine-global store. A per-project
# split would fragment your history and require both to stay installed and
# hooked — the exact thing we warn against.
#
# The switch is ASYMMETRIC, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest:
#   claude-mem -> agentmemory   memories can be POSTed to /agentmemory/remember
#   agentmemory -> claude-mem   claude-mem has NO ingest API; only its own hooks
#                               write. So we export to Markdown (portable, and
#                               what an Obsidian vault wants) and point at the
#                               transcript-replay script. Memories are NOT
#                               silently recreated inside claude-mem.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
memory_dir_default() { printf '%s' "$(pwd)/memory-export-$(date +%Y%m%d)"; }

# Export whichever backend is active. Both produce Markdown with YAML front
# matter and [[wiki-links]], so a vault looks the same either way -- the point
# of a "second brain" is one place holding BOTH the graph (structure) and the
# memories (reasoning). claude-mem ships no export command, so we read its
# SQLite directly, read-only.
memory_export() {
    local out="${1:-$(memory_dir_default)}"
    local here; here="$(dirname "$(command -v agent-tools 2>/dev/null || echo "$0")")"
    [ -n "$PY" ] || { c_bad "no working python"; return 1; }

    local name="memory-export.py"
    [ "$MEMORY_BACKEND" = claude-mem ] && name="claude-mem-export.py"

    local script="$here/$name"
    [ -f "$script" ] || script="$(dirname "$0")/scripts/$name"
    [ -f "$script" ] || { c_bad "$name not found next to agent-tools"; return 1; }

    if [ "$MEMORY_BACKEND" = claude-mem ]; then
        "$PY" "$script" "$out"          # positional outdir
    else
        "$PY" "$script" --out "$out"
    fi
}

memory_stop_agentmemory() {
    # Stop AND disable, so a backend nothing writes to is not left supervised.
    # The STORE is never deleted — switching back must stay possible.
    case "$PLATFORM" in
        macos)
            launchctl bootout "gui/$(id -u)/$SERVICE_LABEL" 2>/dev/null
            [ -f "$PLIST" ] && { mv "$PLIST" "$PLIST.disabled" 2>/dev/null && c_ok "launchd job disabled (plist kept as .disabled)"; } ;;
        linux|wsl)
            has_systemd && { systemctl --user disable --now agentmemory.service 2>/dev/null && c_ok "systemd unit disabled"; } ;;
    esac
    ( cd "$AM" 2>/dev/null && agentmemory stop --force >/dev/null 2>&1 )
    if have claude && claude plugin list 2>/dev/null | grep -q "agentmemory@agentmemory"; then
        # `uninstall` FAILS when the plugin is enabled at PROJECT scope (it lives
        # in a repo's .claude/settings.json, shared with the team). Disabling at
        # local scope always works and stops the hooks immediately, which is what
        # actually matters. Observed 2026-07-29.
        if claude plugin uninstall agentmemory@agentmemory >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            c_ok "agentmemory plugin removed (its hooks no longer fire)"
        elif claude plugin disable agentmemory@agentmemory --scope local >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            c_ok "agentmemory plugin disabled for you (hooks stopped)"
            c_warn "It is still enabled in a project's .claude/settings.json."
            c_warn "Remove \"agentmemory@agentmemory\" from enabledPlugins there so the"
            c_warn "repo does not load a backend you no longer use."
        else
            c_warn "could not disable the plugin — do it manually:"
            c_warn "    claude plugin disable agentmemory@agentmemory --scope local"
        fi
    fi
    c_ok "agentmemory store preserved at $AM (nothing deleted)"
}

memory_switch() {
    local target="${1:-}"
    case "$target" in
        claude-mem|agentmemory) ;;
        *) c_bad "usage: agent-tools memory switch <claude-mem|agentmemory>"; return 2 ;;
    esac
    local current="$MEMORY_BACKEND"
    [ "$current" = "$target" ] && { c_ok "already using $target"; return 0; }

    hdr "Switching memory backend: $current -> $target"
    c_warn "This is machine-wide. Memory spans projects, so it cannot be per-project."

    # 1. Always export first — a portable record that outlives either tool.
    if [ "$current" = agentmemory ] && curl -sf -m 5 http://localhost:3111/agentmemory/health >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        local out="$HOME/agentmemory-export-$(date +%Y%m%d)"
        hdr "1/3 export current memories"
        memory_export "$out" && c_ok "exported to $out"
    else
        hdr "1/3 export current memories"
        c_warn "nothing to export (source backend not reachable)"
    fi

    # 2. Import where an API exists; be explicit where none does.
    hdr "2/3 import into $target"
    if [ "$target" = agentmemory ]; then
        c_warn "claude-mem -> agentmemory: memories can be POSTed to /agentmemory/remember."
        c_warn "Run this after agentmemory is up, against the exported notes."
    else
        # claude-mem has no ingest API, so we go through its own pipeline:
        # synthesise a transcript containing the memories and hand it to the
        # Stop hook. It then summarises and embeds them natively, which is the
        # only way they end up searchable rather than half-imported.
        local conv; conv="$(dirname "$(command -v agent-tools 2>/dev/null || echo "$0")")/agentmemory-to-claude-mem.py"
        [ -f "$conv" ] || conv="$(dirname "$0")/scripts/agentmemory-to-claude-mem.py"
        if [ -f "$conv" ] && [ -n "$PY" ]; then
            c_warn "claude-mem has no ingest API. Converting via its own hook pipeline"
            c_warn "(it SUMMARISES on ingest, so text is compressed, not verbatim —"
            c_warn " the Markdown export above stays the exact record)."
            "$PY" "$conv" --cwd "$(pwd)" --dry-run 2>&1 | sed 's/^/    /'
            if confirm "the converted memories into claude-mem now"; then
                "$PY" "$conv" --cwd "$(pwd)" --go 2>&1 | sed 's/^/    /'
            else
                c_warn "skipped — run later:  $conv --go"
            fi
        else
            c_warn "converter not found; your Markdown export is the record"
        fi
        c_warn "To also replay raw past sessions (optional, costs tokens):"
        c_warn "    scripts/claude-mem-import-transcripts.sh   # dry run first"
    fi

    # 3. Disable the outgoing backend, install the incoming one.
    hdr "3/3 switch over"
    if [ "$current" = agentmemory ]; then memory_stop_agentmemory; fi
    if [ "$target" = claude-mem ]; then
        if cm_installed; then c_ok "claude-mem already installed"
        elif confirm "claude-mem"; then
            npx -y claude-mem install >/dev/null 2>&1 \
              && c_ok "claude-mem installed (wires its own hooks + MCP)" \
              || c_bad "install failed — try: npx claude-mem install"
        fi
    fi
    remember_backend "$target"
    c_ok "backend is now: $target"
    echo "  Restart Claude Code so hook changes take effect."
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Is a run active in THIS repo, and what has it done so far?
status_cmd() {
    local root; root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || { c_bad "not a git repository"; exit 1; }
    local log="$root/.git/graphify-refresh.log"; [ -f "$log" ] || log="$root/graphify-refresh.log"
    hdr "Refresh status — $(basename "$root")"
    if graphify_busy_here "$root"; then
        local pid; pid="$(printf '%s' "${BUSY_WHERE:-}" | sed -n 's/.*pid \([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')"
        local et; et="$(ps -o etime= -p "${pid:-0}" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
        c_ok "RUNNING${et:+ — $et elapsed}${pid:+ (pid $pid)}"
        # Low CPU is normal: it blocks on the LLM, not the processor.
        echo "  note: low CPU is expected — it waits on the model, it does not compute"
    else
        c_ok "no run active in this repo"
    fi
    echo "  graph : $(cd "$root" && graph_stats)"
    if [ -f "$log" ]; then
        echo "  log   : $log"
        local chunks; chunks="$(grep -cE 'chunk [0-9]+/[0-9]+ done' "$log" 2>/dev/null)"
        local total;  total="$(grep -oE 'chunk [0-9]+/[0-9]+ done' "$log" 2>/dev/null | tail -1 | sed 's|.*/||;s| done||')"
        [ "${chunks:-0}" -gt 0 ] && echo "  chunks: ${chunks}${total:+/$total} done"
        echo "  last  :"; tail -3 "$log" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/      /'
    else
        echo "  log   : none (no detached run has been started here)"
    fi
}

# Stop a run in THIS repo only — never another project's.
stop_cmd() {
    local root; root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || { c_bad "not a git repository"; exit 1; }
    if ! graphify_busy_here "$root"; then c_ok "nothing running in this repo"; return 0; fi
    local pid; pid="$(printf '%s' "${BUSY_WHERE:-}" | sed -n 's/.*pid \([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')"
    if [ -z "$pid" ]; then c_bad "could not identify the pid (${BUSY_WHERE:-?})"; return 1; fi
    kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null; sleep 3
    kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null && { kill -9 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; sleep 1; }
    if graphify_busy_here "$root"; then c_bad "still running — check manually"; return 1; fi
    c_ok "stopped (pid $pid)"
    echo "  Completed files stay cached, so re-running resumes rather than restarting."
}

case "${1:-help}" in
    doctor)            doctor ;;
    install-machine)   install_machine ;;
    caveman)           shift; caveman_cmd "${1:-status}" ;;
    update|upgrade)    shift; update_cmd "${1:-all}" ;;
    init)              init_project ;;
    refresh)           shift; refresh "$@" ;;
    status)            status_cmd ;;
    obsidian)          shift; obsidian_export "${1:-}" "${2:-both}" ;;
    memory)            shift
                       case "${1:-status}" in
                         export) shift; memory_export "${1:-}" ;;
                         switch) shift; memory_switch "${1:-}" ;;
                         status) echo "  backend: $MEMORY_BACKEND"
                                 cm_installed && echo "  claude-mem : installed" || echo "  claude-mem : not installed"
                                 am_installed && echo "  agentmemory: installed" || echo "  agentmemory: not installed" ;;
                         *) echo "usage: agent-tools memory <status|export|switch>"; exit 2 ;;
                       esac ;;
    stop)              stop_cmd ;;
    platform)          echo "$PLATFORM" ;;   # used by the self-test
    help|--help|-h)    usage; long_help ;;
    version|--version) echo "agent-tools $VERSION" ;;
    *) usage; exit 2 ;;
esac
